  "worked_solution": "Notice that the $a$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5,$ then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10,$ and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,5a+4b=7\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ (\\,-2a+6b=20\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+8b=14 \\\\ -10a+30b=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10a}+8b & =14 \\\\ -\\cancel{10a}+30b & =100 \\\\ (8b+30b) & =(14+100) \\\\ 38b & =114 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+4b & =7 \\\\ 5a+4(3) & =7 \\\\ 5a+12 & =7 \\\\ 5a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-1$ and $b=3,$ which can be written as $(-1,3).$",
